      "evidence": [
        "N11 lesson.md line 47, bound to p0077#3, identifies the domain as natural numbers N and explicitly describes its abscissas as 1,2,3,...,n,...",
        "The retained p0077#3 page lines contain that same enumeration; this is book-internal evidence, not an external convention",
        "The N11 proper-fraction example begins at 1/2, gives h(1)=1/2, h(2)=1/3 and h(3)=1/4, and explicitly names N as the domain; it corroborates the convention rather than supplying a hypothetical h(0)"
      ],
      "reasoning": "The same book has already supplied the positive-natural-number input convention. Consequently N23 uses n=1,2,3,...; its odd n=2k-1 and even n=2k parametrizations with k in N have positive exponents. The all-real-x domain, origin statement and parity/range properties are consistent with that prerequisite. No definition or value of 0^0 is needed or asserted.",
      "limit": "Not a claim that every use of N in external mathematics excludes zero; no N23 wording changed to positive integer and no new exponent condition inserted",
      "sourceCorrectionRequired": false
    },
