      "observationRetained": "The printed proof explicitly enumerates 0<a<b, a<b<0 and a<0<b; it does not explicitly enumerate a=0<b or a<b=0. This observation is true and is not erased.",
      "reasoning": [
        "For a=0<b, the earlier property 1 gives f(a)=0, and the earlier odd-power sign property 2a gives f(b)>0; hence f(a)<f(b).",
        "For a<b=0, property 2a gives f(a)<0 and property 1 gives f(b)=0; hence f(a)<f(b).",
        "These are substitutions into actual earlier statements in the same lesson, already under the positive-exponent convention, not a new theorem, an invented 0^0 value or task solutions.",
        "Together with the printed three nonzero cases, they cover a<b. For n=1 the required comparison is already the base a<b comparison.",
        "The even-power claim is restricted to negative and positive number sets, so its two same-sign cases do not require a zero-endpoint proof."
      ],
      "sufficiencyDecision": "The omission is expositional rather than a material mathematical/source defect: the lesson itself already states exactly the missing endpoint facts before this proof. A reader can complete those two direct comparisons without additional assumptions. PASS does not claim that the local printed enumeration is literally exhaustive.",
